{"number": 27, "text": "                          ‘comfort          and\n                          Suffixes         and      word       stress       (1)\n\n                          Some  words  are made  up of a root and a suffix:\n\n                                root                                        suffix\n\n                                root                         [ial           suffix\n\n                          In some words  with  suffixes, the stress stays on the same syllable as in the root. Compare:\n                                 ‘danger  and:\n\n                          In other words, the suffix changes the stressed syllable. Compare:\n                                 ‘commerce    and: com'mercial\n\n                          Suffixes which don’t usually change the stress pattern in the root word include -able, -age, -al\n                          (but see Unit 12 for -ial), -er, -ful, -less, -ness, -ous and -fy. For example:\n                                 ‘comfort —                        per'cent — per'centage            e'lectric — e'lectrical\n                                 ‘amplify —                        re'gret — re'gretful              re'gard — re'gardless\n                                 ‘foolish — 'foolishness           di'saster — di'sastrous           ‘beauty —\n                                Exceptions  with -able and -al include:\n                                ad'mire  —                         pre'fer —\n                                 ‘medicine — me'dicinal            ‘agriculture — agri'cultural\n\n                          Note  that before the suffixes -ious, -ulous, -orous and -eous main stress usually comes in the\n                          syllable before the suffix:\n                                 ‘industry — in'dustrious          'mystery —  my'sterious\n                                 ‘miracle — mi'raculous            ‘carnivore — car'nivorous\n                                 ad'vantage — advan'tageous        ‘outrage — out'rageous\n                     A47\n                          Some  suffixes themselves usually have the main stress. These include -ee, -eer, -ese and -ette.\n                          For example:\n                                               irefu'gee\n                                 Japan'ese  —_, Nepal'ese                        —di'skette\n                                 Exceptions include: 'omelette, ‘etiquette, em'ployee (although less commonly  we use\n\n                   A      Note: Some  people say ‘cigarette.\n\n                          Words  with these suffixes can often have stress shift (see Unit 10):\n                                 She’s japanESE.   but: She’s a JAPanese\n                                 He’s a refuGEE.   but: We  saw  photos of REFugee   CHILdren.\n\n                          You  can find more practice of words with  suffixes in Section E3.\n\n\n                28           English Pronunciation in Use (Advanced)", "char_count": 2928, "mean_conf": 86.15, "word_count": 268, "words": [{"text": "‘comfort", "x0": 493, "y0": 127, "x1": 802, "y1": 192, "conf": 84, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 835, "y0": 128, "x1": 964, "y1": 192, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "‘comfortable", "x0": 1008, "y0": 127, "x1": 1471, "y1": 192, "conf": 0, "bold": false}, {"text": "Suffixes", "x0": 486, "y0": 223, "x1": 779, "y1": 287, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "and", "x0": 814, "y0": 224, "x1": 943, "y1": 287, "conf": 95, "bold": false}, {"text": "word", "x0": 982, "y0": 223, "x1": 1156, "y1": 287, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "stress", "x0": 1194, "y0": 228, "x1": 1399, "y1": 287, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "(1)", "x0": 1437, "y0": 218, "x1": 1529, "y1": 300, "conf": 96, "bold": false}, {"text": "A4S:", "x0": 391, "y0": 361, "x1": 461, "y1": 404, "conf": 44, "bold": false}, {"text": "Some", "x0": 486, "y0": 368, "x1": 583, "y1": 401, "conf": 96, "bold": true}, {"text": "words", "x0